.TH INITWAIT 1M "16 November 2026" "amix-base"
.SH NAME
initwait \- change the run level, and see that init does it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /usr/sbin/initwait
.I level
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B initwait
asks
.B init
for the run level
.I level
(0 to 6,
.B s
or
.BR S )
as
.BI "/sbin/init " level
does, then watches the run level
.B "who \-r"
shows until it changes.
.PP
.B init
takes such a request at once, but after heavy file writing on a machine
with little memory it may act on it only at its next periodic check, up to
five minutes later.
Meanwhile the system runs on as before, and the shell prompt is back.
.B initwait
waits up to 30 seconds for the change.
When there is none, it says so in one line and sends the request once more,
which
.B init
acts on at once:
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
init has not acted on the request for run level 0 in 30 seconds:
asking again
.ft R
.fi
.PP
(one line on the machine).
When the run level still has not changed after another 30 seconds,
.B initwait
says that it has not, and that
.B init
will act at its next check, and exits.
It does not halt the machine itself: the run level's own steps
.RB ( /sbin/rc0
for 0 and 5,
.B /sbin/rc6
for 6) still run first.
.PP
When the run level cannot be read, or already is
.IR level ,
.B initwait
sends the request and returns at once, as
.B /sbin/init
does.
When the change comes in time,
.B initwait
says nothing; the run level's own steps usually end it.
.SS shutdown
The base package's
.B /usr/sbin/shutdown
(also
.B /sbin/shutdown
and
.BR /etc/shutdown )
is the system's own script with one change: its last step,
.BI "/sbin/init " state ,
is
.BI "/usr/sbin/initwait " state .
Its options, messages and checks are the system's, as
.BR shutdown (1M)
describes them.
.B man shutdown
shows this page, since the base package gives
.BR shutdown ;
the system's page is
.BR "/usr/bin/man shutdown" .
After
.B shutdown
or
.BR initwait ,
switch the machine off only when the system says it has halted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the run level changed, or nothing was watched; 1 when it did not
change in the time given, or the usage was wrong; otherwise the status of
.BR /sbin/init .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B INITWAIT_SECS
the seconds to wait for the change after each request; default 30.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /sbin/init
.TP
.B /sbin/who
.SH BUGS
.B init
can also stall after it has taken the new run level, before the level's
own steps start: for 0,
.B "who \-r"
already shows the new level and the console says
.BR "INIT: New run level: 0" ,
but
.B /sbin/rc0
does not start.
.B initwait
does not see this, since the level has changed, and sending the request
again does not end it: on the emulated 16 MB machine with heavy file
writing it was seen on some boots, ending by itself after minutes or not
at all.
Switch the machine off only when the system says it has halted.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR init (1M),
.BR shutdown (1M),
.BR who (1)
